Right-to-left and mixed text, as a browser shows it (Unicode Bidirectional Algorithm, UAX #9). A platform shaper gives
a line's glyphs in visual order: the right-to-left runs reversed, the glyph x positions out of text order, and, when
the first strong letter is right-to-left, the whole line laid out as a right-to-left paragraph. Wrapping, painting and
hit tests assume that x grows with the byte index, so a wrapped line lost its width: a fragment drew at the window's
left edge and the rest of the paragraph was missing. Now `layout_wrapped_line` shapes a line that holds right-to-left
text in its paragraph direction (a direction mark before it when its first strong letter has the other one), puts the
glyphs into text order with each cluster's advance (`bidi::logical_layout`), wraps that, and lays out each row in
visual order (`bidi::BidiRows`, with `unicode-bidi`). A wrapped line paints its glyphs, underlines, strikethroughs and
backgrounds at those places, and finds an index from a point and a point from an index there. Text without
right-to-left letters keeps its old layout. The paragraph direction is the new `TextStyle::direction`
(`TextDirection`, `Styled::text_direction`, `WindowTextSystem::shape_text_in`), left-to-right unless set, as HTML
without `dir`. A text element's hit tests also take its `text_align` into account (`WrappedLineLayout::row_offset`, which
the bidi rows are also painted at), as its painting does, and `TextLayout::closest_index_for_position` gives the
nearest character boundary as a selection takes it. `first_strong_direction` gives a text's direction as HTML's
`dir="auto"` takes it, and `WrappedLineLayout::bidi_glyph_origins` where a line in the bidi path paints each glyph.
Greek, Armenian, Hebrew, Arabic, Syriac and Thaana are word characters for wrapping, so a line breaks between their
words, not inside them. Our own.

